{
  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "blockIndex": 0,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Along a slender cord, string, or rod, a wave travels mainly along its length.\nIt can travel either way along this axis."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "blockIndex": 1,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "A gas, liquid, or solid can form a continuous medium.\nA local disturbance can spread in many directions within it.\nThe source and medium set the pattern through local interactions."
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "blockIndex": 2,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Propagation through a region adds some new features.\nWe now examine spreading and the shape of wavefronts."
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "blockIndex": 3,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "An outward-moving wave can spread over a growing region.\nThe circular water ripples in Figure 64 illustrate this spreading."
    },
    {
      "index": 4,
      "blockIndex": 3,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "They are surface waves, not spherical waves inside the water.\nThe energy from the source is shared over a widening front."
    },
    {
      "index": 5,
      "blockIndex": 3,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "Energy crossing unit area in one second measures energy flux.\nFor steady, uniform spreading with no added energy, this flux falls with distance."
    },
    {
      "index": 6,
      "blockIndex": 3,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "With other conditions fixed, small-oscillation energy scales with amplitude squared.\nThis is the relation discussed in Section 11, so a falling flux means a smaller amplitude."
    },
    {
      "index": 7,
      "blockIndex": 3,
      "paragraphInBlock": 4,
      "text": "It applies to small oscillations of a spring mass, a pendulum, and local medium particles."
    },
    {
      "index": 8,
      "blockIndex": 4,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Geometric spreading can reduce amplitude even in a lossless medium.\nThis decrease needs no conversion of mechanical energy into internal energy."
    },
    {
      "index": 9,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "An **ideal plane wave** does not undergo geometric spreading.\nA large thin plate moving normally can approximate such a wave."
    },
    {
      "index": 10,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Assume a uniform, isotropic elastic medium and uniform driving.\nPoints touching the plate then oscillate with equal amplitude and phase.\nThe wave travels normal to the plate."
    },
    {
      "index": 11,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "Points on each plane parallel to the plate have the same phase.\nAn equal-phase surface is called a **wavefront**.\nPlane-wave wavefronts are planes (Figure 73)."
    },
    {
      "index": 12,
      "blockIndex": 5,
      "paragraphInBlock": 3,
      "text": "A finite plate gives only an approximation away from its edges.\nNear an edge, diffraction bends the wavefronts."
    },
    {
      "index": 13,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "**A wave ray is a line normal to a wavefront.**\nFor a plane wave, these normals form parallel straight lines."
    },
    {
      "index": 14,
      "blockIndex": 8,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "In a uniform, isotropic medium, rays also show the propagation direction.\nIn this ray description, wave energy travels along the rays."
    },
    {
      "index": 15,
      "blockIndex": 9,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "An ideal plane wave keeps a fixed cross-sectional area as it travels.\nIn a uniform lossless medium, its amplitude therefore stays constant."
    },
    {
      "index": 16,
      "blockIndex": 9,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Dissipation can reduce it, while real finite beams may also spread at their edges."
    },
    {
      "index": 17,
      "blockIndex": 10,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Straight water-surface waves provide a visible analogy to plane waves.\nTo produce them, let a rod lightly touch the water and move it up and down."
    },
    {
      "index": 18,
      "blockIndex": 10,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Away from the rod ends, points on lines parallel to it have nearly equal phase (Figure 74).\nThese are surface wavefronts, not plane shear waves in the liquid bulk."
    },
    {
      "index": 19,
      "blockIndex": 15,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "A uniformly pulsating sphere can produce **spherical waves** (Figure 75).\nIn a uniform, isotropic medium, the outgoing wavefronts are spherical."
    },
    {
      "index": 20,
      "blockIndex": 15,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "They are spheres centered on the source.\nThe rays point outward along the extensions of the radii."
    },
    {
      "index": 21,
      "blockIndex": 16,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "For steady, uniform spherical spreading without absorption, each sphere carries the same power."
    },
    {
      "index": 22,
      "blockIndex": 16,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Larger spheres have more area, so less energy crosses each unit of area in one second.\nWith the medium and frequency fixed, the amplitude decreases with radius."
    },
    {
      "index": 23,
      "blockIndex": 16,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "This is geometric spreading, which occurs even without absorption."
    },
    {
      "index": 24,
      "blockIndex": 17,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "In a **transverse wave**, particle displacement is perpendicular to propagation.\nA transverse bulk wave in a solid involves **shear deformation**."
    },
    {
      "index": 25,
      "blockIndex": 17,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "Layers shift relative to one another.\nIn the small, pure-shear model, there is no volume change to first order.\nIn an elastic solid, shear produces restoring forces."
    },
    {
      "index": 26,
      "blockIndex": 17,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "These forces drive the local oscillations."
    },
    {
      "index": 27,
      "blockIndex": 18,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "Here, ordinary liquids and gases have no static shear stiffness.\nThey cannot carry bulk elastic shear waves in this model.\nSolids can carry them, with the surface qualification in ①."
    },
    {
      "index": 28,
      "blockIndex": 19,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "① Liquid-surface waves can move particles both vertically and horizontally.\nGravity and surface tension restore the surface, not bulk shear elasticity."
    },
    {
      "index": 29,
      "blockIndex": 20,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "A **longitudinal wave** has displacement along propagation (Section 37).\nIn a solid, it involves compression and extension."
    },
    {
      "index": 30,
      "blockIndex": 20,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "In an ordinary liquid or gas, it involves compression and rarefaction.\nThis does not require the fluid to sustain tensile stress."
    },
    {
      "index": 31,
      "blockIndex": 20,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "Bulk compression produces restoring effects in all these media.\nSolids, liquids, and gases can therefore carry longitudinal waves."
    },
    {
      "index": 32,
      "blockIndex": 21,
      "paragraphInBlock": 0,
      "text": "In the usual isotropic elastic model of rock, longitudinal P waves travel faster than transverse S waves.\nA station therefore normally records P waves first."
    },
    {
      "index": 33,
      "blockIndex": 21,
      "paragraphInBlock": 1,
      "text": "S waves from the same earthquake arrive later.\nTheir arrival-time difference helps estimate the distance to the source."
    },
    {
      "index": 34,
      "blockIndex": 21,
      "paragraphInBlock": 2,
      "text": "The estimate requires suitable P- and S-wave speeds and a model of their paths.\nOne station's delay alone does not uniquely locate the source."
    }
  ],
